          "text": "The main difference is **what we optimize**. “Distributed” describes how agents coordinate.\n\n| | Standard k-means | Our disk model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Assignment | Each dot belongs to its nearest center | Weight is split among covering disks; uncovered dots go to the nearest center |\n| Objective | Minimize total squared distance to centers | Balance loads while limiting intersections |\n| Variables | Center positions | Center positions **and radii** |\n| Constraints | No overlap graph required | Connected disk graph, degree ≤6 |\n\n**K-means does not aim for equal cluster loads.** Dense clusters can contain many more dots than sparse ones.\n\nOur latest strict self-improvement and neighbor-annealing rules are also experimental choices, not standard k-means updates.\n\nIf we remove the disks and assign every dot to its nearest center, then seek balanced loads while keeping distances small, we are moving toward **balanced or capacity-constrained k-means**. That may be the simpler comparison to focus on.",
