              "diff": {
                "text": "% Insert before \\end{document} in local_mesh_balancing.tex.\n\\section{Compact hexagonal cells: simulation protocol}\n\\label{sec:compact-hexagon-protocol}\n\nThe next experiment uses 100 cells. The earlier choice of 38 matched\nthe clustering baseline; it was not an optimized cell count.\nKeep the same data, movement proposals, acceptance rule, and geometric\nconstraints. Use a finer initial mesh. The median score below controls\ntermination only, not acceptance of movements.\n\n\\paragraph{Data and coverage.}\nUse the original 5,986 taxi pickups from 15 January 2015,\n08:00--08:15, and the verified Manhattan main-island boundary with\nits hole retained. Assign the 5,983 inside-domain records exactly once.\nRetain and flag the three outside records. Record input identifiers,\nboundary version, coordinate transform, and random seeds.\n\nLet $D$ denote the island, $H_i$ an underlying convex six-sided cell\nin the shared-vertex mesh, and $S_i=H_i\\cap D$ its service region.\nAll 100 service regions must have positive area, cover $D$ completely,\nand have no positive-area overlaps. Shared borders are allowed.\nUse a documented deterministic assignment convention for pickups on\nshared borders. Require, for every underlying and clipped cell,\n\\[\n \\frac{\\operatorname{diam}(A)}{w_{\\min}(A)}\\leq 2,\n \\qquad A\\in\\{H_i,S_i\\},\n\\]\nwhere $w_{\\min}$ is the minimum projection width over all directions.\nClipped service regions need not have six sides.\nRepair initial geometry before balancing, without silently relaxing\nthe cell count, coverage, or shape constraints.\n\n\\paragraph{Median termination rule.}\nLet $L_i$ be the load of cell $i$, and $N_i$ its neighbors sharing a\npositive-length service-region border inside Manhattan. Point contact\nalone does not establish adjacency. For $L_i>0$, define\n\\[\n g_i=\\operatorname{median}_{j\\in N_i}\n       \\left(100\\,\\frac{|L_i-L_j|}{L_i}\\right),\n \\qquad\n M=\\operatorname{median}_{i=1,\\ldots,100} g_i.\n\\]\nEach percentage uses the focal cell's own load as denominator.\nFor an empty cell, set $g_i=+\\infty$ if any neighbor has positive\nload, and $g_i=0$ if all neighbors are empty. Report an empty neighbor\nset as an invalid adjacency state rather than excluding that cell.\nInclude all 100 cells, including infinite scores. For an even number\nof values, the median is the average of the two middle sorted values.\n\nReport the initial score separately. Stop successfully at the first\ncompleted balancing round with $M\\leq5\\%$. A manual stop, round cap,\ntime limit, or small movement does not establish convergence under\nthis rule. The median condition permits empty or poorly balanced\nindividual cells; it does not certify balance everywhere.\n\n\\paragraph{Test control before starting.}\nCheck that the execution environment works and that no unintended\nduplicate solver is running. Demonstrate a short test that produces\na map, saves a resumable checkpoint, and stops on command.\nVerify checkpoint retrieval and resumption before starting the full\nexperiment. Display the initial map before balancing.\n\n\\paragraph{Progress and snapshots every minute.}\nDuring initialization, repair, and balancing, publish progress and a\nsaved map at least once per minute. Identify the run, timestamp,\nstage, completed rounds, current score, best completed-round score\nand its round, empty-cell count, and actual solver status.\nReport coverage, overlap, assignment, and shape checks with their\naudit timestamp; distinguish older audits from current measurements.\nMap labels show loads. Captions distinguish initial, intermediate,\nmanually stopped, and converged states. If a snapshot is taken during\na round, label it and identify the last completed round used for the\ntermination score.\n\nUse short interruptible work batches so that a long round or repair\noperation cannot block reporting or stopping. Save consistent\ncheckpoints atomically, retaining geometry, ownership, random-number\nstate, update order, and the within-round position needed for exact\ncontinuation. Keep timestamped snapshots and a latest checkpoint.\nMonitoring reads saved state without restarting or duplicating the\nsolver.\n\n\\paragraph{Stop control and failure reporting.}\nProvide a stop control checked between bounded work batches.\nOn request, preserve the latest consistent state, cease further\nupdates, and return a final map with confirmed process status.\nLabel manual stopping separately from convergence.\nAn active conversation indicator is not evidence of solver progress:\nverify the process and advancing logs or checkpoints.\nIf execution, snapshot retrieval, or monitoring fails, report the\nfailure explicitly instead of claiming continued progress.\n",
