        {
          "type": "agentMessage",
          "id": "d4a04811-8f4d-5843-a506-82acb40ce74b",
          "text": "Here is a precise **proposed rule for ordinary moves**. Departure–insertion stays as agreed.\n\n**1. Freeze the weight model**\n\nAt the start of the selected cell’s turn:\n\\[\n\\rho_j=\\frac{W_j}{A_j},\\qquad\n\\widehat W_j(q)=\\rho_j A_j(q).\n\\]\nDensities stay fixed throughout the move. No post-move pickup counts enter its acceptance test.\n\n**2. Define neighborhood balance**\n\nFor each cell \\(j\\), use its mean absolute neighbor-weight gap:\n\\[\nG_j(q)=\\frac1{|N_j(q)|}\n\\sum_{k\\in N_j(q)}\n|\\widehat W_j(q)-\\widehat W_k(q)|.\n\\]\n\nFor moving cell \\(i\\), fix the scoring set at the start:\n\\[\nS_i=\\{i\\}\\cup N_i(0),\\qquad\nE_i(q)=\\sum_{j\\in S_i}G_j(q).\n\\]\n\nThus we protect the moving cell **and its original neighbors**. Keep that set fixed so before/after scores are comparable.\n\n**3. Select the cell and move type**\n\n- Select cell \\(i\\) with probability proportional to its current \\(G_i\\), with replacement.\n- If every \\(G_i=0\\), select uniformly.\n- Apply the departure test first. If it qualifies, departure–insertion consumes its whole turn.\n- Otherwise choose **balance with probability 0.9**, **circularity with probability 0.1**.\n\n**4. Ordinary movement**\n\nOnly center \\(i\\) moves; other centers remain fixed.\n\n| Turn | Direction | Acceptance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Balance | Descent direction of \\(E_i\\) | \\(E_i\\) decreases |\n| Circularity | Ascent direction of \\(C_i=4\\pi A_i/L_i^2\\) | \\(C_i\\) increases and \\(E_i\\) does not increase |\n\nRequire these protections **along the whole segment**, not merely at its endpoint. At a nondifferentiable point, use a descent direction if one exists; otherwise leave the center unchanged.\n\n**5. Geometric limits**\n\nStay inside the domain, avoid coincident centers, and end the move at the **first adjacency-change event**, then refresh neighbor information. Simultaneous changes need a separate convention; I propose stopping just before them.\n\nTwo choices deserve explicit agreement:\n\n- **Neighborhood sum versus median:** I recommend the sum for movement decisions; keep medians for reporting.\n- **Direction:** descending \\(E_i\\) changes the old proposal direction. It directly targets neighborhood balance, rather than proposing a self-improving move and merely checking neighbor damage afterward.\n\nThere is no balance–circularity exchange rate in this formulation."
